<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Odo of France</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 857–898</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> · Also known as Odo, Count of Paris, Eudes, King of West Francia · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robertians">Robertians</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Odo was Count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> and King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-francia">West Francia</a> from 888 to 898. As count he led the defence of Paris during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> siege of 885 and 886, and the nobles chose him as king after the deposition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-fat">Charles the Fat</a>, the first king of West Francia from outside the Carolingian family. A member of the Robertian family, he was a forerunner of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capetian-dynasty">Capetian dynasty</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/odo-of-france.jpg" alt="Odo of France" width="320" height="322" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Makers of the 13th-century Grandes Chroniques de France · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coronation_of_Odo.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Odo was the eldest son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-the-strong">Robert the Strong</a>, Duke of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a>, Margrave of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neustria">Neustria</a> and Count of Anjou, who died at the Battle of Brissarthe in 866. Because Odo and his brother Robert were still young, King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-bald">Charles the Bald</a> put Hugh the Abbot in charge of the counties their father had held. Count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> from 882, Odo was also lay abbot of St <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-of-tours">Martin of Tours</a>. In 882 or 883 he married Théodrate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/troyes">Troyes</a>.</p><p>A large army of Northmen sailed up the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seine">Seine</a> in July 885 and reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> on 24 November. Odo and Bishop Joscelin, who had refortified the city, beat off an assault, and a siege followed. After the bishop died on 16 April, Odo made a sortie to seek aid. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-fat">Charles the Fat</a>'s commander Henry of Alemmania died trying to relieve the city, and Charles paid the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Vikings</a> to move on to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>. Evidence suggests that Odo's rise came through alignment with the emperor, whose court he visited.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-fat">Charles the Fat</a> was deposed at the end of 887 in a revolt led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arnulf-of-carinthia">Arnulf of Carinthia</a>, leaving a power vacuum in the Carolingian empire. Odo had inherited the property of Bishop Joscelin and Hugh the Abbot and held military command of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neustria">Neustria</a> and three lay abbacies. The West Frankish nobles chose him as king, and Walter, Archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sens">Sens</a>, crowned him at Compiègne in February 888. Fulk, Archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reims">Reims</a>, resisted and invited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guy-iii-of-spoleto">Guy of Spoleto</a> to take the crown, without success. Odo defeated a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> force at Montfaucon, and Arnulf then recognised him. A second coronation followed at Reims in November.</p><p>His power was limited outside his family lands, and he rarely left northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. He gave his brother Robert most of his honours, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poitiers">Poitiers</a>. From 892 a party in favour of Charles formed around Fulk and others, and revolts broke out in the south and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a>. Fulk crowned the fourteen-year-old <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-simple">Charles the Simple</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reims">Reims</a> in 893 with Pope Formosus's support. Odo won a surprise attack that autumn and besieged Reims, and Arnulf backed first Charles and then Odo. After negotiation Odo ceded part of the realm to Charles in 896.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>A definitive peace followed in which Odo made Charles his successor. Odo died at La Fère on 1 January 898, and Charles succeeded him. His reign marked the definitive separation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-francia">West Francia</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-empire">Carolingian Empire</a>, which was never reunited. He was the first king of the Robertian dynasty, the parent house of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capetian-dynasty">House of Capet</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Odo of France” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Robertian family had led the defence of Neustria against the Vikings since Robert the Strong in the 860s, and two of its members, <b>Odo</b> and Robert I, had briefly been kings.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-the-franks">Duchy of the Franks</a></li><li><blockquote>Count <b>Odo of Paris</b> defended the city against the Vikings in 885 to 886 and was elected king, beginning a rivalry between Carolingians and his Robertian family.</blockquote><a href="/state/west-francia">West Francia</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Neustria. Died in La Fère, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=49.66,3.37&amp;year=883" aria-label="La Fère on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Odo of France"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5186 5875h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5186 5875h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=49.66,3.37&amp;year=883">What was here in 883 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
